  "era": "Older concrete-block industrial shed (1950s per the architects' text), converted in 2006 (Lea Katz Architecture)",
  "source": {
   "title": "ArchDaily: Pack Line / Lea Katz Architecture. Dimensioned ground level plan, pre-conversion plan, elevations and link-bridge section",
   "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/78779/pack-line-lea-katz-architecture",
   "license": "Published plan, used only as a reference for dimensions"
  },
  "assumptions": [
   "Scale 25.4 px/m from the plan's own figures (overall depth 36.12 m, grids 9.47 / 9.62 m); walls read to about +-0.3 m",
   "Box height 7.0 m: the drawings give the parapet at +8.58 and a 7.00 m cladding field, not the clear height under the roof trusses; 7.0 m is assumed (anything from about 6.5 to 8 m is possible)",
   "Outer walls: 'concrete blocks wall' behind 'perforated steel panel' (SW elevation 07ee); the block taken as 20 cm (block_20, 16 dB at 5 GHz, table) plus 10 dB estimated for the skin, so loss_db 26 at 5 GHz on every facade. No measurement of the panels exists; a fine perforation acts like a mesh and could block 20 dB or more. Inside the box this only changes the yard, which is left out of the shares",
   "East wall windows: two rows (sill 1.58 m and 5.58 m, 1.0 m high, 4.6 m in each bay) read from the two mullioned type-3 panel rows of the NE elevation (07f0); single glazing assumed, the perforated skin in front drawn as shutter 'slits' (10 dB, the nearest table entry)",
   "No windows drawn on the north, south and west walls: the plan and the SE / SW elevations show none",
   "Loading and exit doors are steel (door_steel) and closed; the interior storage double door is open",
   "Gallery over the south half of the steel-work room: extent from the pre-conversion plan (07f2), whose stair is still on the 2006 plan; deck at 2.8 m, drawn as a 0.25 m 'column' (concrete) box = 20 dB at 5 GHz, the floor-slab row of the materials table",
   "Office / service strip: steel-frame pods 2.95 m high (link-bridge section 07f8), with an upper level on a deck at 2.95 m (also 20 dB) that the link bridge reaches at +3.03; above 3.2 m the strip is open to the hall (railing assumed)",
   "Pod walls gypsum_double, showers block_10, dining front glass, exit lobby block_10: assumed from the line weights of the plan",
   "Welding zone has no wall to the hall; its edge is drawn as a full-height open passage only so it can carry its own label",
   "Cabinet spines (detail B6: steel drawers, 1.43 x 2.77 m) as metal_cabinet; packing machines in assembly as the table's 'machine' (solid metal, 10 dB/m capped at 25 dB), so a partly open steel frame may block less; machine height 1.8 m assumed; no own loss values are set on any obstacle",
   "Storage racks loaded with steel profiles drawn as racking_full (capped at 25 dB); bundles of steel bar can block more",
   "Furniture not drawn on the plan is typical: parts washer and shelving in the cleaning zone, welding tables, lockers in the changing room, a server rack in the computer office",
   "AP1 on the roof trusses at 6.8 m (ceiling - 0.2 m) over the aisle between the A/B cells and the C/D machines, near the middle of the building's length (west of the hall's own centre, to reach the storage and steel-work block too), where an installer would hang the first one",
   "Storage / steel-work wall taken as concrete 20 (concrete_20): the plan gives only its 0.21 m thickness and the pre-conversion plan shows it as an existing wall",
   "The west loading apron under the canopy and the two-storey office building to the south are outside the box"
  ],
  "israeli_features": [
   "Shed in the Holon industrial zone. The architects' text (gated on the ArchDaily page, seen only as a quoted search snippet) calls it a 1950s shed used by five firms over 55 years, with its non-structural concrete walls removed",
   "Old concrete-block walls kept and wrapped in a new skin of perforated steel panels (types 1-3 on the elevations, 'concrete blocks wall / perforated steel panel' on the SW elevation)",
   "One 0.21 m wall of the old plan kept between storage and steel work",
   "Steel-frame office and service pods along the south wall, with a two-storey link bridge to the office building",
   "No protected space (mamak / mamam) is drawn: old sheds usually rely on a shared public shelter"
  ],
  "notes": "This is an old (1950s, per the architects) concrete-block shed with a perforated steel skin, not the usual steel-portal and sandwich-panel unit, so the outer walls stop more Wi-Fi than the inside walls but much less than solid steel cladding. One AP in the middle covers the open assembly field, but the rows of steel cabinet spines and machines cast long shadows, and the storage, steel-work room, front wing and south office strip sit behind block or concrete walls and need their own APs. The model has no reflections: in a real steel-filled hall the far ends can be up to about 15-18 dB stronger than shown, so treat the dark far corners as a pessimistic estimate (+-9 dB in a metal hall)."
 },
